# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PromptQlConfig — turns this supergraph into a PromptQL-enabled project
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is the global config that tells DDN which LLM powers PromptQL's planner
# and its AI primitives (summarize / classify / extract / visualize), plus the
# `systemInstructions` that steer how the planner uses YOUR tools. It lives at
# globals/metadata/promptql-config.hml in a standard DDN layout; it is kept here
# under ddn/globals/ for this repo.
#
# ⚠️ MODEL STRINGS ARE POINT-IN-TIME. The exact model identifiers below
#   (claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929) were current when this was written and WILL
#   drift — Anthropic publishes dated snapshots that get superseded. Before
#   deploying, replace them with a current Anthropic model id from the docs.
#   Anthropic is the recommended provider for PromptQL.
#
# The apiKey is read from an environment variable at runtime (never hard-code a
# key here); set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in your DDN env / secrets.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

kind: PromptQlConfig
version: v2
definition:

  # ── LLM that drives PLANNING (turning a question into a query plan). ──
  llm:
    provider: anthropic
    model: claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929   # point-in-time — verify/replace before deploy
    apiKey:
      valueFromEnv: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY

  # ── LLM that backs the AI PRIMITIVES (executor.summarize / classify /
  #    extract / visualize) the plan calls. Can match `llm` or differ. ──
  aiPrimitivesLlm:
    provider: anthropic
    model: claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929   # point-in-time — verify/replace before deploy
    apiKey:
      valueFromEnv: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY

  # ── Steering for the planner. This is where we tell PromptQL HOW to use the
  #    retrieval-bridge tools — reinforcing the `description` fields on the
  #    search_documents command and the SearchHit.account relationship. ──
  systemInstructions: |
    You answer questions about support tickets.

    RETRIEVAL: When the user describes an incident, symptom, error, or asks to
    find / look up / gather similar or past tickets, ALWAYS use the
    `search_documents` command to retrieve candidate tickets first. It runs
    hybrid (semantic + exact-keyword/BM25) search, so include any literal error
    codes, ticket IDs, or plan tiers from the question verbatim in the `query`
    so exact tokens match. Use the `status` argument (e.g. "resolved") and
    `plan_tier` argument to filter when the user implies them, and keep `top_k`
    small (5–10) — this step narrows the corpus to a handful of candidates to
    reason over, it is not the whole answer.

    JOINING FACTS: To get account / customer context for a retrieved ticket
    (project name, plan tier, monthly revenue, region, seat count), follow the
    `account` relationship on each SearchHit — do not try to look those up
    separately. Use plan_tier and monthly_revenue from `account` when the user
    wants results prioritised or ranked by customer value.

    REASONING: After retrieving, use the AI primitives where judgment is needed
    (classify the root cause of a ticket, summarize its fix) and do the rest —
    filtering, joining, ranking — deterministically. Treat retrieval as a tool
    inside the plan, not as the final answer.
